---
title: Culling what can’t be reached
---
Find the parts of a map that can’t be reached, then delete them or put them to use [[t:222-223 t:230-234 s:24]].

## The rule
```text
find the open tile nearest the center
flood outward from it, counting steps: a Dijkstra map
every open tile the flood never reached is unreachable
mark those tiles, or turn them to wall
```

The talk builds this on Dijkstra maps: from a starting point, every walkable tile gets the number of steps to it, and a tile left at the starting value can’t be reached [[t:214-221 s:23]].

## Step through it
@@steps limit=4

## Controls
@@control mode
`mark` shows the unreachable tiles and leaves them, as the talk’s first demo of it does [[c16:68-69]]. `remove` turns them to wall, as its later demos do before they place anything [[c17:56-65]].

## What it produces, measured
@@measured

Marking changes no tile, so this is the cave map’s own row [[own]]. With `remove`, as in the talk’s next demo, a map is {{c17.regions.max}} region on every seed [[run:c17.regions.max]]. What the cull costs varies: the floor share after it runs from {{c17.floor_share.min}} to {{c17.floor_share.max}}, where the caves before it never fell below {{c03.floor_share.min}} [[run:c17.floor_share.min c17.floor_share.max c03.floor_share.min]]. The talk’s warning is the extreme of this: a map with only two tiles left to walk on [[t:238-240]].

## Where it fails
@@fails

These are the cave map’s failures, which marking leaves in place [[own]].

## What it combines with
@@combines

The talk culls before it picks a start, so the player can’t be placed somewhere they can’t get out of [[t:236-237]].

